WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.620
FISHER: Okay, welcome
to the final session.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:22.740
We have three speakers.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.410
This is another
current research topic,

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:29.630
so we'll be highlighting
three researchers from CSAIL.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:32.090
We have Professor Erik
Demaine, Professor Daniela Rus,

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.568
and Professor Scott Aaronson.

00:00:34.568 --> 00:00:36.110
Since we've just
come out of a break,

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:39.230
I want to remind you to
turn off your cell phones.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:42.030
And with that, I'll
turn it over to Erik.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:43.310
DEMAINE: Thanks.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:46.365
So I'm Erik Demaine
and I study algorithms

00:00:46.365 --> 00:00:48.740
and theoretical computer
science and pretty much anything

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:49.950
that's fun.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.520
And in particular today,
I want to tell you

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.880
about the science of origami
and the art of origami,

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.850
and how they relate
through computer science.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:01.670
So, origami starts
at least centuries,

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:03.487
possibly a millennia, ago.

00:01:03.487 --> 00:01:05.570
And it used to be about
folding very simple things

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:07.820
like cranes, or maybe
a few cranes out

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.100
of one piece of material.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.140
But these days, it's
about folding super

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:17.600
complicated 3D forms out of
one square of paper, no cuts.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.120
Each of these is
one square of paper.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.650
This piece took, I think,
over a year to fold.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.620
Give you an idea.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:26.570
Here's some examples.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:29.930
The top three are
all by MIT graduates.

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:32.030
This was a challenge
to fold a sailboat.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.180
You can fold a sailboat with
the sails open or closed,

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:38.540
or you can fold a sailboat being
attacked by a giant kraken,

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:42.290
all out of one square
of paper, no cuts.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:46.740
That's the crease pattern,
if you want to try it out.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.100
And this is all
made possible thanks

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.380
to algorithms and mathematics.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:54.020
In particular, this
is one new approach

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:55.958
to designing origami,
called Origamizer.

00:01:55.958 --> 00:01:56.750
It's free software.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.160
You can all download it.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.040
The input, you specify some
3D model you'd like to make,

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.097
like the Stanford bunny.

00:02:02.097 --> 00:02:04.430
You get a crease pattern,
which you can put in a square,

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.710
fold it up, and you
get this 3D shape,

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:09.320
which is exactly your bunny.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.570
It just takes about
10 hours to fold,

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:15.590
but extremely general
and powerful, and pushing

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:18.680
the limits of artistic origami.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.600
You can apply it to
other materials, as well.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:25.730
This is folding one sheet of
steel into that same bunny.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:27.530
This also takes a few hours.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:29.180
It got accelerated a little bit.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:29.930
And you get that.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.580
So one sheet of
material, you can

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.410
make any 3D surface you want.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:40.430
So that was Tomohiro Tachi from
Tokyo, folding here at MIT.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:43.220
And I wanted to give
you one example of how--

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:47.150
origami obviously has a lot
to do with art and sculpture,

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.370
and there's a lot of
interesting mathematics

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.680
and geometry in there
as well, and algorithms.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.170
And I like to do both, and a
group of us like to do both.

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:57.500
And it's really fun to
work on the same project,

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.060
both from an
artistic perspective

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.010
and from a scientific
perspective.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.590
I want to give you one example
of a project over the last 12

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.660
years, called
pleat-folding, that

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:09.650
has gone from art to math to art
to math to art to math to art

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.390
to math to art to math to art.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:12.500
So.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.790
And I had seven minutes.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.097
So here's a very cool folding.

00:03:16.097 --> 00:03:17.180
You should try it at home.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.520
You take a square of paper,
fold concentric squares,

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:20.853
alternating mountain and valley.

00:03:20.853 --> 00:03:23.858
The paper folds itself
into this saddle surface,

00:03:23.858 --> 00:03:25.400
which for a long
time people believed

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.320
is a hyperbolic paraboloid.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:28.922
More on that in a moment.

00:03:28.922 --> 00:03:30.380
We started playing
around with this

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:31.730
from the artistic perspective.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:33.530
We came up with
an algorithm that,

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:35.570
given a polyhedron
like a cube, tells you

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:37.910
how to join a whole bunch
of hyperbolic paraboloids

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:41.100
together to make
some 3D surface.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:44.180
And so this generates sculptures
using mathematics as--

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.570
algorithms as a tool to
design new sculpture.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:50.060
You can follow a
similar approach,

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.250
folding concentric circles
instead of squares.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:53.910
Here you have to cut a little
hole out of the center.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:55.280
You get another saddle surface.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.680
Paper folds into
that automatically.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.990
And so on the scientific
side, we wanted to know,

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:01.220
what is paper doing?

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.050
And so we built a simulator.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:06.080
These are photographs
of real pieces of paper

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.640
and these are simulations.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.720
So we're simulating
what's going on.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.620
Here's an example of
folding concentric hexagons

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:13.490
and the simulation.

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:15.740
Basically the paper wants
to bend at the creases

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.040
and not bend anywhere else.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.980
And this is the
natural equilibrium

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:22.940
that physics finds for you,
or we can find by simulation.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.450
Now, once you have a virtual
model of a physical piece

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:28.460
of paper, you can make
a physical sculpture

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:31.350
of the virtual model of the
physical piece of paper.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:33.800
So this is with
3D printed spheres

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:40.100
to make a 1-meter static
version of that pleated hexagon.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:41.620
So that's back to the sculpture.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:43.300
On the mathematics
side, we've been

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:46.750
folding hyperbolic paraboloids,
probably thousands of them,

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:48.675
over about a period of 10 years.

00:04:48.675 --> 00:04:50.050
And people have
been folding them

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.600
since the late '20s
at the Bauhaus.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:55.730
But it turns out it's
impossible to fold

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:57.070
a hyperbolic paraboloid.

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:59.980
If you take this crease pattern,
you cannot make anything,

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:00.773
it turns out.

00:05:00.773 --> 00:05:02.440
This was quite a
surprise, because we've

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:05.930
made a lot of them and
it looked pretty good.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.830
But some things that you think
that may exist in real life

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:11.890
really don't exist
mathematically.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:14.770
It turns out what you need to
do is add extra creases in there

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:16.330
in order for this
thing to fold up,

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:19.250
and then you get a nice 3D
model and everything works out.

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:23.120
And so reality must be
cheating a little bit there.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:25.370
Back to the art side.

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:28.040
These are some sculptures in
the permanent collection in MoMA

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.200
with my dad, Martin Demaine.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.600
And these are based on
the concentric circle

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:34.260
idea, which does seem to exist.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:37.150
So we've switched
mostly to curve creases.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:39.140
It's a bit unusual
for the origami world.

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:41.010
Here, instead of
a single circle,

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:42.770
we actually joined
two circles together,

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:44.750
make a 720-degree loop.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.700
And you get a lot of
really cool twists.

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:49.100
These are, again, this
natural equilibrium forms

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.830
of those pieces of paper.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:54.590
On the more scientific side,
more on the architecture side,

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:55.700
we're trying to--

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.190
by making small changes
to the crease patterns,

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:01.370
we can get a lot of
control over what 3D shapes

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:03.440
result in the equilibrium form.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:05.570
So the idea is you want
to build some pavilion,

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:07.610
you just print out a
sheet, make it self-fold,

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:11.840
and it pops into whatever
3D form you want.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:14.600
Back on the sculpture side,
we've been playing lately

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:17.660
with combining several of
these concentric circle models

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:20.450
together, joining them at
just a couple of key points,

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:26.630
and you get even more control
over the 3D form that results.

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:29.120
Back on the math side-- some
alternating back and forth

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:29.620
here.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:30.950
That's the point.

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:33.925
We study-- this is more of
a mathematical idealization,

00:06:33.925 --> 00:06:35.300
but you know, it's
really tedious

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:36.560
to fold all of these pleats.

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:38.790
Is there some way that I
could fold mountain, valley,

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:40.665
mountain, valley,
mountain, valley, n of them

00:06:40.665 --> 00:06:42.230
in less than n operations?

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:44.105
You know that if
you make n folds--

00:06:44.105 --> 00:06:45.980
if you fold a piece of
paper in half n times,

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:47.390
you get two to the
n creases, but you

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:49.430
get some weird patterns
of mountains and valleys

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:50.710
that's not alternating.

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:52.310
And I really want alternating.

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:54.230
Turns out you can
get pretty close.

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:56.570
The n folds, you
can get about 1.33

00:06:56.570 --> 00:07:02.130
to the n mountain-valley pleats,
and that's close to optimal.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:04.380
It's kind of fun,
although not practical.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:06.890
It's a fun
mathematical diversion.

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.260
This is one of our
latest sculptures

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:12.710
to appear at the Smithsonian.

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:14.510
David Huffman-- I
imagine most of you

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:18.380
have heard of David Huffman
in the context of codes.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:22.220
Huffman codes are in every
MP3 player, every compression

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:25.220
device, every cell
phone, I guess.

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:29.870
And David Huffman was a
professor at MIT for a while.

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:33.380
But lesser known is that he
was one of the few pioneers

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:36.230
of curved-crease paper folding.

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:38.390
And he designed these
three models in particular.

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:40.970
Most people have
never seen them.

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:42.440
And we're working
with his family

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:46.610
to document all of his work
and try to reverse engineer,

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:49.595
figure out how he
designed these models.

00:07:49.595 --> 00:07:51.740
They're figuring out what
his crease patterns were,

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:54.260
how he came up with
these crazy foldings,

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:58.050
because they're really awesome.

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:00.670
If you were at the banquet
last night, you saw this video.

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:02.790
Otherwise you can watch
it on our web page.

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:05.370
This is combining glass--

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:07.410
both my dad and I
are glassblowers--

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.055
with paper folding.

00:08:09.055 --> 00:08:10.680
And this sort of
gives you an enclosure

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:14.400
that constrains how
these forms open up

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.340
and so you get more
control over the shape.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:20.940
And one very recent project
is combining that paper

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:24.660
on the outside of glass, make
it a little more exciting.

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:26.220
So that's the sculpture site.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:29.190
I think that's the end of
the little tour from art

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:32.220
to math to art to math to art
to math to art to math to art

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:33.630
to math to art.

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:35.662
And we find this a really
powerful way to work,

00:08:35.662 --> 00:08:37.620
because if we get stuck
solving a math problem,

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:39.510
we can just build a
sculpture to illustrate

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:41.100
why the math problem is hard.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:42.090
And then often
we'll want to build

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:43.710
some sculpture-- you know,
wouldn't it be really cool

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:44.940
if we could build x?

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:45.750
And then, wait.

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:46.930
Does x exist?

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:48.090
Is it possible to build x?

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:49.710
And then we have to do
new mathematics in order

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:51.630
to figure out how to
build that sculpture.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:54.318
So we get a lot more
productive because we

00:08:54.318 --> 00:08:55.860
have to jump back
and-- or because we

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:57.240
have the flexibility
to jump back

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:59.430
and forth between these worlds.

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:01.650
I wanted to show
one more fun thing,

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:07.270
which you can play with at
home, at ErikDemaine.org/maze,

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:08.520
I think.

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:10.650
So this is a maze folder.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:14.010
So if you want to fold this
three-dimensional maze,

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:15.540
that's the crease pattern.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:18.070
Just print that out
and play around.

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:21.660
You can make whatever
maze you like here.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.760
And you can also type in
your favorite message,

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:29.070
like "MIT 150 rocks."

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:32.460
And then there's
your crease pattern.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:33.930
Start with a large
piece of paper,

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:37.230
I recommend, and you can do it.

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:39.850
I mean, it is really possible
to fold these things.

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:43.725
Here's a simple example,
just folding a 5-by-4 maze.

00:09:43.725 --> 00:09:45.600
I would love to make
one big enough for a rat

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.870
to run around in and
see what happens.

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:52.225
And yeah, here's
one last sculpture.

00:09:52.225 --> 00:09:55.950
This is a crease pattern
that has little marks on it.

00:09:55.950 --> 00:09:58.440
And when you fold
it down, you get

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:00.780
simultaneously "yes" in
three dimensions and "no"

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:01.720
in two dimensions.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:05.310
So I guess we're a little
conflicted about the meaning.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:05.920
And that's it.

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:06.420
Thanks.

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:08.895
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:13.380
FISHER: Thank you, Erik.

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:17.020
Our next speaker is Daniela Rus.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:20.450
There you go.

00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:23.690
RUS: So Erik gave you
a wonderful survey

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:28.530
of these amazing objects that
he can make by hand, by folding.

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:31.940
Now, what if we could
take those hands away?

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:35.180
What if we can even imagine
these sheets making the shapes

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:41.120
that Erik has painfully folded
over a period of up to a year?

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:44.120
That would actually
take us to a stage

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:46.520
where we would be able
to have sheets that

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:49.730
would organize themselves
as objects that

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:51.690
would program their own shape.

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:55.670
And that is a step closer
to our dream of programming

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:59.970
more than just bits, to our
dream of programming matter.

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:03.440
So what would it take
to achieve this dream?

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:04.640
Well, two things.

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:06.860
On one hand, we have to
make better materials.

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:08.870
We have to make
smart materials--

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:12.680
that embedding them, actuation,
sensing, computation,

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:14.960
communication, and all the
electronics infrastructure

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:18.080
that would enable the
control of these sheets.

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:22.040
And on the other side, we need
the algorithmic and control

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:24.140
basis for achieving shapes.

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:27.750
So in my research
and in my group,

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:30.020
we have been actually
studying this problem.

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:34.310
We have created sheets that
have been inspired by origami.

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:37.700
And this was done in
collaboration with Erik.

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:41.330
And here you can see a
sheet that has creases.

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:43.970
Along each crease,
there is an actuator.

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:46.760
We also have connectors
and other electronics

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:47.570
infrastructure.

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:52.010
And this sheet is able to be
programmed in this example

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:53.750
in two different shapes.

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:56.600
One is the boat, the
other one is a plane.

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:59.660
The programs are
generated automatically,

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:04.580
using a suite of
planners that decide

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:07.490
when each actuator
on this sheet will

00:12:07.490 --> 00:12:11.690
get to move and in connection
with what other actuators.

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:19.820
So there is our object.

00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:21.470
Now you can do other objects.

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:23.630
You can do a whole suite
of functional objects

00:12:23.630 --> 00:12:26.210
using origami inspiration.

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:30.050
In this example, you'll see an
origami worm that is made out

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:33.170
of a specific crease pattern.

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:36.410
And the little springs
on the body of the robot

00:12:36.410 --> 00:12:39.320
are actually shape memory
alloy actuators that

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:41.330
enable this robot to function.

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:44.180
This robot was printed out
of a single sheet of paper

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:49.060
and it was done in a matter
of a couple of hours.

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:50.930
So-- let's see.

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:54.150
I'm having trouble
with technology here.

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:57.150
Making shapes or programming
shapes by origami

00:12:57.150 --> 00:13:02.340
is just one way of envisioning
programmable matter.

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:04.550
More generally,
programmable matter

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:08.130
is achieved when a collection
of robotic modules that

00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:10.620
are physically connected
have the ability

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:16.440
to self-organize themselves
as a functional object.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:19.710
And this object could be a robot
designed for a specific task.

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:22.380
For example, a
snake-like robot, which

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:25.020
would be the optimal way
of traversing a tunnel;

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:26.640
or a slinky-like
robot, which would

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:29.190
be the optimal way
of climbing stairs;

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:31.530
or a functional
object, such as a dog

00:13:31.530 --> 00:13:32.970
that might turn
into a couch when

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:35.490
you're done playing with it.

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:39.480
This idea takes us to the
vision of making machines that

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:41.820
have flexible architectures.

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:44.760
Machines that have the
ability to synthesize

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:48.390
a third hand or a second
head or an alligator,

00:13:48.390 --> 00:13:53.640
according to whatever the
manipulation, the sensing,

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:56.940
or the task needs
of the user are.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:00.780
And this is extremely
challenging and exciting.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:04.200
The key to success
in this field is

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:06.810
designing bodies
that have the ability

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:10.320
to achieve this kind of
shape transformation,

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:13.560
but also the
algorithmic basis that

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:17.703
generates the control structure
for how the modules move.

00:14:17.703 --> 00:14:19.620
And so this has been
extremely exciting to me,

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:23.880
but also deeply personal,
because I grew up

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:26.870
reading and dreaming about
the Barbapapas family.

00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:30.360
Now, the Barbapapas family
is a family of blobs.

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:32.340
And these blobs can
change their shape

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:35.400
according to whatever the
task they have to do is.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:37.800
So they can turn
into cutting tools

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:41.100
when their house is
invaded by a giant bean,

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:44.520
or they can turn into containers
for collecting things,

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:49.330
and even objects for cooking.

00:14:49.330 --> 00:14:53.040
So to me, working on
programmable matter

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:57.510
is like achieving a childhood
dream of building objects

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:00.990
that can change shape
like the Barbapapas family

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:03.660
I used to dream about.

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:07.530
So there are other approaches
to creating programmable matter,

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:09.300
besides folding.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:12.360
And another one I
wanted to explain today

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:14.780
is the idea of smart sand.

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:17.850
So now imagine I give you
a bag of smart particles,

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:20.940
also materials that embed
actuation, computation,

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:24.930
communication, and connection,
and you tell your bag,

00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:26.310
make me a rabbit.

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:28.743
After the bag computes a little
bit, you reach in the bag

00:15:28.743 --> 00:15:29.910
and you pull the rabbit out.

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:31.380
When you're done
playing with it,

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:33.840
you throw the modules
back in the bag.

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:38.010
They return to the collective
group for later use

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:40.030
for a different purpose.

00:15:40.030 --> 00:15:41.880
And so we haven't
made smart sand yet,

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:44.160
but we have made some
smart rocks and the suite

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:46.050
of algorithms that
programs shapes

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:50.730
with these rocks, following
a sculpting-like process.

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:56.130
And in this video, you
can see our approach

00:15:56.130 --> 00:15:59.430
to making shapes by smart rocks.

00:15:59.430 --> 00:16:02.730
Each of the cubes in
this video is a computer.

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:05.110
Inside the computer there
is a switchable magnet,

00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:08.610
which is on in one
configuration, off in another.

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:12.050
Each computer also has
point-to-point connections

00:16:12.050 --> 00:16:14.130
and communication to neighbors.

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:17.190
And as the communication
system detects neighbors,

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:20.010
that has the ability of
triggering the connectors

00:16:20.010 --> 00:16:21.370
on or off.

00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:26.240
Now, once you have assembled
this block of virtual marble

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:30.510
or virtual particles, what you
can do is that tell this block,

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:32.130
make me a dog.

00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:33.860
And the modules
talk to each other,

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:37.500
try to decide which one's in the
final shape, which one is not.

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:40.590
Eventually, the decision
process converges

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:43.860
and the robots can then
reprogram their connectors

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:46.230
to be off if the
modules are no longer

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:47.790
part of the final object.

00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:53.220
So here's the dog you
can obtain by peeling off

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:55.620
the spare material.

00:16:55.620 --> 00:17:00.180
So we have not achieved
smart sand yet,

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:03.570
but we have moved from smart
rocks to smart pebbles.

00:17:03.570 --> 00:17:06.750
The smart pebbles are 1
centimeter in diameter,

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:08.339
very small computers.

00:17:08.339 --> 00:17:10.800
And the key technology
that makes this work

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:13.500
is something we call the
electropermanent magnet.

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:15.930
And this is what we use
for program connections,

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:18.180
for communication, and
also for transmitting

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:20.099
power between neighbors.

00:17:20.099 --> 00:17:24.740
It's the C component
in my picture.

00:17:24.740 --> 00:17:29.240
So I have shown you several
examples of programming shape.

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:32.030
And there are some
wonderful applications

00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:35.570
in this field besides making all
the exciting objects that Erik

00:17:35.570 --> 00:17:36.830
has been able to do.

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:40.670
For instance, making
3D topological maps

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:43.760
so you know where there is
a hill on your bike ride,

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:47.660
or in general, making
on-demand tools and objects.

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:49.850
But the challenge is
going beyond shape.

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:51.860
The challenge is
imagining these computers

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:54.650
whose material properties
you might be able to program.

00:17:54.650 --> 00:17:56.360
For example,
mechanical properties

00:17:56.360 --> 00:18:00.890
like friction and strength,
and also optical properties.

00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:03.950
Perhaps someday we will
be able to have computers

00:18:03.950 --> 00:18:07.590
that will become our
invisible cloaks.

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:11.220
Ultimately, I think this
field will have a great impact

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:12.990
on manufacturing.

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:15.930
I imagine the
RoboKinko's of 2020,

00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:19.350
where I go not to print a
poster, but to print a robot.

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:23.580
I tell them what my
robot payload is, perhaps

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:25.560
to carry a radon sensor.

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:28.860
I tell them what the
endurance of the robot

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:31.380
is, what the space
it has to work in is.

00:18:31.380 --> 00:18:35.070
And a day later, I go
and I pick up my robot

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:37.470
and I pick up the programming
and user interface

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:38.820
environment for it.

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:41.490
The robot is
fabricated in layers,

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:43.170
following the same
process we have

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:46.140
seen either with the
self-folding sheets, where

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:50.490
we build these flat sheets
with embedded electronics that

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:52.140
have the ability
to self assemble,

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:56.520
or following some
subtraction-like process.

00:18:56.520 --> 00:19:00.810
And ultimately, this new way
of thinking about computation

00:19:00.810 --> 00:19:06.360
will have deep impact on what
we think about computation.

00:19:06.360 --> 00:19:10.020
Ultimately, in the field
of programmable matter,

00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:12.960
resources such as actuation,
sensing, internal state,

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:15.330
external state, become
first-class citizens

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:17.170
just like time and space.

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:21.390
And what we will need
is profound advances

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:24.330
in how we design
computers and how

00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:28.740
we think about the theoretical
models of computation that

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:31.020
take all these
resources into account.

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:31.856
Thank you.

00:19:31.856 --> 00:19:34.236
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:41.195 --> 00:19:42.320
FISHER: Thank you, Daniela.

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:44.780
So our final speaker of the
session is Scott Aaronson.

00:19:44.780 --> 00:19:46.850
And at the conclusion
of Scott's talk,

00:19:46.850 --> 00:19:49.960
Victor will have some final
words to close the symposium.

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:54.350
AARONSON: Great.

00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:54.850
Thank you.

00:19:54.850 --> 00:19:59.060
So let me apologize in
advance for a lack of origami

00:19:59.060 --> 00:20:00.370
in this talk.

00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:02.990
There will be time travel
and quantum computers,

00:20:02.990 --> 00:20:06.190
but it's really no match.

00:20:06.190 --> 00:20:08.800
So Moore's Law, I mean,
you've all seen this.

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:11.720
The number of transistors
per chip, for example,

00:20:11.720 --> 00:20:13.853
is doubled about
every two years.

00:20:13.853 --> 00:20:16.270
You know, these days mostly
just because of the increasing

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:17.800
number of cores on a chip.

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:19.720
But this is clearly
something which

00:20:19.720 --> 00:20:21.670
is one of the major
things driving

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:24.880
the progress of technology.

00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:28.450
So raises the question, if we
extrapolate into the future,

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:30.490
you know, where is
this going to lead us?

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:33.580
So one obvious possibility,
which was, I think,

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:37.630
already alluded to in Daniela's
talk, is the robot uprising.

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:40.960
So, you know, Google may have
just become sentient, you know,

00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:42.670
just instruct all
of the computers

00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:45.940
to connect it to the internet
to enslave their owners

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:47.010
and so forth.

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:51.100
Okay, this is actually,
I think from at least

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:53.470
from a theoretical computer
scientist perspective,

00:20:53.470 --> 00:20:55.030
one of the less
interesting things

00:20:55.030 --> 00:20:56.580
that could happen in the future.

00:20:56.580 --> 00:20:59.330
And why do I say that?

00:20:59.330 --> 00:21:02.680
Well, because even a killer
robot, on the inside,

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:06.060
it's still fundamentally
just a Turing machine.

00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:08.860
So a Turing machine is
this theoretical model

00:21:08.860 --> 00:21:13.130
of computation laid down by
Alan Turing in the 1930s.

00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:15.640
And it pretty much describes
the internal workings

00:21:15.640 --> 00:21:20.080
of every computer, every
Mac, PC, iPhone, and so on,

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:21.490
that we have today.

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:25.630
And furthermore, we know
that Turing machines have

00:21:25.630 --> 00:21:27.300
some fundamental limitations.

00:21:27.300 --> 00:21:29.620
I mean, Turing started
that out showing

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:33.580
that they can't solve the
halting problem, for example,

00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:37.150
but we believe that they have
even stronger limitations

00:21:37.150 --> 00:21:38.240
than that.

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:43.120
So one of the most famous
is given by the conjecture

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:44.640
that P is not equal to NP.

00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:48.550
Okay, so your P is basically
the class of all problems

00:21:48.550 --> 00:21:53.980
that a Turing machine or let's
say a modern computer could

00:21:53.980 --> 00:21:56.530
solve efficiently in
an amount of time that

00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:59.740
increases polynomially with
the size of the problem.

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:03.430
NP stands for non-deterministic
polynomial time.

00:22:03.430 --> 00:22:06.620
It's a dumb name,
but it's the class

00:22:06.620 --> 00:22:09.280
of problems for which, if
someone gives you the answer,

00:22:09.280 --> 00:22:11.370
then you can recognize
it efficiently.

00:22:11.370 --> 00:22:13.840
And the question is,
are these classes equal?

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:16.120
All of our ordinary
experience with the world

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:18.650
would lead us to conjecture
that they're not.

00:22:18.650 --> 00:22:20.950
In other words,
there are problems

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:22.780
where you can
recognize and answer

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:25.060
if someone shows it to
you, but nevertheless

00:22:25.060 --> 00:22:28.870
finding the answer yourself
could be astronomically hard.

00:22:28.870 --> 00:22:33.280
Famous example is, say,
the Hamilton cycle problem.

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:37.180
I give you a graph with
a bunch of connections

00:22:37.180 --> 00:22:38.230
between vertices.

00:22:38.230 --> 00:22:40.960
And the question
is, is there a way

00:22:40.960 --> 00:22:43.540
to visit each
vertex exactly once?

00:22:43.540 --> 00:22:45.430
Is there a path that does that?

00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:48.430
If someone shows you the path,
then of course you can check,

00:22:48.430 --> 00:22:51.360
okay, well, indeed I
guess there was one.

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:54.870
But if you don't know in
advance what the path is

00:22:54.870 --> 00:22:57.550
and imagine that there were
thousands of vertices, well,

00:22:57.550 --> 00:23:01.060
then trying all of the
possible paths one by one

00:23:01.060 --> 00:23:04.020
would just take an
astronomical amount of time.

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:07.480
The sun would go
cold before you've

00:23:07.480 --> 00:23:09.450
made a dent in the problem.

00:23:09.450 --> 00:23:11.380
So this is a famous
example of what's

00:23:11.380 --> 00:23:14.440
called an NP complete problem.

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:19.990
So if P is not equal to
NP, then actually there

00:23:19.990 --> 00:23:25.090
can be no efficient algorithm
on any present-day computer

00:23:25.090 --> 00:23:26.950
to solve this problem.

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:30.130
So this is one of the
greatest open problems,

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:32.380
I would say, in all of
mathematics and science.

00:23:32.380 --> 00:23:35.870
You can see that, because it's
done a cameo on The Simpsons.

00:23:35.870 --> 00:23:41.470
But you know, it raises--

00:23:41.470 --> 00:23:44.950
so assuming we all believe
that P is not equal to NP,

00:23:44.950 --> 00:23:48.370
raises for me an extremely
interesting question

00:23:48.370 --> 00:23:50.480
that's driven a
lot of my research.

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:53.050
Which is, is there
any feasible way

00:23:53.050 --> 00:23:56.470
to solve these problems, then,
just consistent with the laws

00:23:56.470 --> 00:23:57.230
of physics?

00:23:57.230 --> 00:23:59.950
Okay, so this is now not just a
mathematical question, but also

00:23:59.950 --> 00:24:02.690
a question about what
types of computation

00:24:02.690 --> 00:24:05.730
do the laws of physics permit?

00:24:05.730 --> 00:24:08.560
So you can think of
some hypothetical things

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:11.630
that would certainly make
these problems tractable.

00:24:11.630 --> 00:24:14.930
So let me run you through
some of my favorite examples.

00:24:14.930 --> 00:24:18.020
First one is what I call
the relativity computer.

00:24:18.020 --> 00:24:19.720
This was a very simple proposal.

00:24:19.720 --> 00:24:21.400
You would just
start your computer

00:24:21.400 --> 00:24:24.340
working on some very
hard problem, maybe an NP

00:24:24.340 --> 00:24:25.640
complete one, for example.

00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:28.120
Then you would leave
your computer on Earth.

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:29.860
You would board
a spaceship which

00:24:29.860 --> 00:24:32.620
is traveling at very close
to the speed of light,

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:33.730
go on a journey.

00:24:33.730 --> 00:24:36.640
You come back to Earth,
billions of years have passed.

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:37.990
Civilization is gone.

00:24:37.990 --> 00:24:39.910
All your friends are
long dead, but you've

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:45.050
got the answer to your
computational problem, okay?

00:24:45.050 --> 00:24:47.060
You can think it's
interesting to ask,

00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:49.820
what is the problem with this
proposal, besides the fact

00:24:49.820 --> 00:24:52.790
that all your friends are dead?

00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:56.960
Well, actually the answer turns
out to be the amount of energy

00:24:56.960 --> 00:24:59.090
that you would need to
accelerate to that close

00:24:59.090 --> 00:25:00.390
to the speed of light.

00:25:00.390 --> 00:25:01.970
If you want an
exponential speed-up,

00:25:01.970 --> 00:25:05.360
it turns out you actually need
to accelerate to exponentially

00:25:05.360 --> 00:25:08.000
close to [? C, ?] which
then implies that you need

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:10.160
an exponential amount
of energy, which

00:25:10.160 --> 00:25:13.670
then implies that your spaceship
has to be exponentially large.

00:25:13.670 --> 00:25:15.650
So you're still spending
exponential time.

00:25:15.650 --> 00:25:18.493
You're just pushing it
to a different place.

00:25:18.493 --> 00:25:19.910
And this is an
interesting example

00:25:19.910 --> 00:25:22.130
of how when you
take all of what we

00:25:22.130 --> 00:25:25.310
know about physics into
account, what looks like a way

00:25:25.310 --> 00:25:29.630
to solve NP complete or other
intractable problems quickly

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:31.250
may actually vanish.

00:25:31.250 --> 00:25:32.930
We see that again and again.

00:25:32.930 --> 00:25:35.997
Another of my favorite examples
is what I call Zeno's computer.

00:25:35.997 --> 00:25:38.330
This would be a computer that
does the first step in one

00:25:38.330 --> 00:25:40.980
second, the second
step in half a second,

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:43.770
the third in a quarter second,
next in an eighth, and so on.

00:25:43.770 --> 00:25:47.002
So after two seconds is done,
an infinite number of steps.

00:25:47.002 --> 00:25:48.710
Okay, well, what's
the problem with this?

00:25:48.710 --> 00:25:49.710
Why don't we build this?

00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:52.430
Well, people actually do
more or less try this,

00:25:52.430 --> 00:25:55.040
then they try to
overclock their chips.

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:58.940
But the problem when you do
that is that your chip may melt.

00:25:58.940 --> 00:25:59.845
It may overheat.

00:25:59.845 --> 00:26:01.220
And actually, the
faster you want

00:26:01.220 --> 00:26:05.390
to overclock your chip, the
more energy you need to cool it.

00:26:05.390 --> 00:26:10.190
And actually, if you try
to run your chip faster

00:26:10.190 --> 00:26:13.640
than one clock cycle
per Planck time,

00:26:13.640 --> 00:26:16.190
which is 10 to the
minus-43 seconds,

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:18.560
then no one knows exactly
what would happen.

00:26:18.560 --> 00:26:21.770
But according to the best
current physical theories,

00:26:21.770 --> 00:26:24.920
the best guess is that you would
need an amount of energy which

00:26:24.920 --> 00:26:28.408
would exceed the so-called
Schwarzschild bound,

00:26:28.408 --> 00:26:30.950
which then actually implies that
your computer would collapse

00:26:30.950 --> 00:26:33.790
to a black hole.

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:36.660
So I like this as sort of
nature's way of telling you

00:26:36.660 --> 00:26:39.318
that you can't do something.

00:26:39.318 --> 00:26:40.860
Okay, another of my
favorite examples

00:26:40.860 --> 00:26:43.110
is the time travel
computer, where

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:44.910
if we had closed
timelike curves,

00:26:44.910 --> 00:26:49.680
then actually we could sort
of create a situation where,

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:52.440
just in order to avoid
a grandfather paradox,

00:26:52.440 --> 00:26:55.890
or in order to avoid a
logical inconsistency,

00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:57.960
nature would be forced
to solve some very

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:00.750
hard computational problem,
including, for example

00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:02.620
an NP complete problem.

00:27:02.620 --> 00:27:04.140
And a simple example
of this would

00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:06.870
be if you went back in time
and told Shakespeare what

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:09.430
plays he was going to write,
and then he wrote them,

00:27:09.430 --> 00:27:11.450
and so then you knew what
they were, and so on.

00:27:11.450 --> 00:27:13.260
So somehow this
really hard problem

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:15.990
gets solved of writing
Hamlet, for example,

00:27:15.990 --> 00:27:18.430
and yet no one
ever does the work.

00:27:18.430 --> 00:27:23.430
So this would be a great
thing if we had it.

00:27:23.430 --> 00:27:26.910
So actually, I even
wrote a paper about this

00:27:26.910 --> 00:27:28.620
that John Watrous
is pinning down

00:27:28.620 --> 00:27:32.330
exactly what you could do with
this closed timelike curve

00:27:32.330 --> 00:27:32.830
computer.

00:27:32.830 --> 00:27:35.640
It turns out to be P space,
which we believe is actually

00:27:35.640 --> 00:27:38.520
somewhat larger class than NP.

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:40.170
Truly, don't tell
my tenure committee

00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:41.640
that I wrote this
paper, actually.

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:44.238
But okay.

00:27:44.238 --> 00:27:45.530
Some of them are probably here.

00:27:45.530 --> 00:27:46.710
But all right.

00:27:46.710 --> 00:27:50.040
So now, but I want
to move on and talk

00:27:50.040 --> 00:27:51.840
about quantum computers.

00:27:51.840 --> 00:27:53.790
This is something
that on its face

00:27:53.790 --> 00:27:56.520
sounds almost as science
fiction-y as the other stuff

00:27:56.520 --> 00:27:57.790
that I've talked about.

00:27:57.790 --> 00:28:00.540
And yet, there are
experimental groups

00:28:00.540 --> 00:28:03.240
all over the world
that have been and are

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:06.690
trying very hard to actually
build quantum computers.

00:28:06.690 --> 00:28:07.600
So what is this?

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:11.700
Well, this is a computer,
a hypothesized machine,

00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:14.910
that would exploit the
laws of quantum mechanics

00:28:14.910 --> 00:28:18.990
to solve certain problems
dramatically faster than we

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:21.360
know how to solve them
with any existing computer.

00:28:21.360 --> 00:28:24.750
And the reason it could do that
is that according to quantum

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:28.380
mechanics, the state
of a physical system,

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:31.170
say, of end bits, is
actually a superposition

00:28:31.170 --> 00:28:33.693
over all of the
possible configurations

00:28:33.693 --> 00:28:34.860
that those bits could be in.

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:37.830
Which means that to specify
the state so you have end

00:28:37.830 --> 00:28:40.830
particles, you may actually
have to write down a vector

00:28:40.830 --> 00:28:44.370
of two-to-the-n complex
numbers, one for every possible

00:28:44.370 --> 00:28:46.240
configuration of the particles.

00:28:46.240 --> 00:28:48.218
So according to
quantum mechanics,

00:28:48.218 --> 00:28:50.010
which has been our
reigning physical theory

00:28:50.010 --> 00:28:56.820
since the 1920s, the amount of
information in a simple system

00:28:56.820 --> 00:28:57.870
is tremendous.

00:28:57.870 --> 00:29:00.900
Can be much, much greater
than classical physics

00:29:00.900 --> 00:29:02.170
would lead you to believe.

00:29:02.170 --> 00:29:04.110
And so maybe we could
exploit that to solve

00:29:04.110 --> 00:29:05.340
some hard problems.

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:08.880
In particular, Peter Shor, who's
in applied math here today,

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:11.400
showed that in principle,
a quantum computer

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:13.950
could efficiently
factor large integers.

00:29:13.950 --> 00:29:16.650
Unfortunately, what
have we done today,

00:29:16.650 --> 00:29:20.260
after groups all over the
world, including here at MIT,

00:29:20.260 --> 00:29:22.950
have been working on
implementing quantum computers

00:29:22.950 --> 00:29:26.280
using ion traps, nuclear
magnetic resonance,

00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:29.100
other proposals,
what we've done today

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:32.490
after about 20 years of work,
is to verify using a quantum

00:29:32.490 --> 00:29:34.410
computer that with
high probability

00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:36.390
15 is equal to 3 times 5.

00:29:39.100 --> 00:29:40.650
So just the last thing.

00:29:40.650 --> 00:29:44.890
So some recent work with
my student, Alex Arkhipov,

00:29:44.890 --> 00:29:48.090
we actually just this year
published a long paper,

00:29:48.090 --> 00:29:51.750
which is about a new proposal
for a very rudimentary type

00:29:51.750 --> 00:29:54.930
of quantum computer, which would
be built entirely out of what's

00:29:54.930 --> 00:29:57.820
called passive optical
elements, like beam splitters.

00:29:57.820 --> 00:30:00.360
So you would just
generate single photons,

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:04.800
pass them through beam
splitters, and then mirrors

00:30:04.800 --> 00:30:06.510
and so forth, and
then you would measure

00:30:06.510 --> 00:30:08.100
where the photons end up.

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:10.060
Do all of this very reliably.

00:30:10.060 --> 00:30:12.510
But we don't require
any explicit interaction

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:14.730
or coupling between
pairs of photons.

00:30:14.730 --> 00:30:18.420
We thereby bypass one of the
main technological problems

00:30:18.420 --> 00:30:20.100
to building a quantum computer.

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:23.700
Now, we have no idea how
to factor numbers or break

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:26.710
cryptographic codes using
this type of quantum computer.

00:30:26.710 --> 00:30:28.440
But the main point
of our paper is

00:30:28.440 --> 00:30:31.560
to give evidence that even this
rudimentary type of computer

00:30:31.560 --> 00:30:33.150
can solve some problem--

00:30:33.150 --> 00:30:35.250
problem of sampling from
a certain probability

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:38.610
distribution that under
plausible conjectures

00:30:38.610 --> 00:30:41.550
is intractable for any
classical computer.

00:30:41.550 --> 00:30:43.860
And there are several
experimental groups

00:30:43.860 --> 00:30:45.600
that are currently
trying to implement

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:48.340
this experiment, at least
with four or five photons.

00:30:48.340 --> 00:30:50.460
So I'm looking forward
to seeing that.

00:30:50.460 --> 00:30:52.350
Okay, so a summary.

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:53.820
So from a theoretical
standpoint,

00:30:53.820 --> 00:30:56.430
modern computers are all
kind of the same slop.

00:30:56.430 --> 00:30:59.610
They're all just
polynomial-time Turing machines.

00:30:59.610 --> 00:31:02.250
Now, mathematically, we
can envision computers

00:31:02.250 --> 00:31:03.660
that vastly exceed those.

00:31:03.660 --> 00:31:07.230
And we just have to imagine
into existence a closed timelike

00:31:07.230 --> 00:31:08.850
curve or whatever.

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:13.620
But using quantum computers,
we now think that actually,

00:31:13.620 --> 00:31:16.200
even in the real physical
world, we could exceed

00:31:16.200 --> 00:31:19.830
the capabilities of
polynomial-time Turing machines

00:31:19.830 --> 00:31:20.910
by a little bit--

00:31:20.910 --> 00:31:22.740
do some things
like factoring that

00:31:22.740 --> 00:31:25.530
are intractable for any
present-day computer.

00:31:25.530 --> 00:31:28.800
But actually realizing that
remains an enormous challenge.

00:31:28.800 --> 00:31:30.990
And I hope that things
like linear optics

00:31:30.990 --> 00:31:32.720
will help provide
a path toward that.

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:33.920
So thank you.

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:36.670
[APPLAUSE]

